  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nA trough that was located across KY/West TN/AR last evening will \nbe making slow eastward progress today, crossing our area late in \nthe day. Weak dynamics with the trough will mean generally similar\nsensible weather to what we have had for the past several days. \nWeak flow, PWAT values in the 1.8 to 2 inch range, and MLCAPE \nvalues of 1000-1500 J/kg point to another day with a potential for\nscattered convection with a few storms becoming strong to \nmarginally severe, with strong winds the primary threat along with\nlocalized flooding.\n\nWith the trough east of our area by Monday, we will be in a NW flow \nthrough the mid to upper levels. This will bring some midlevel \ndrying and lower PW, but also greater instability with lapse rates \n6.5-7 C/km and MLCAPE in the 1500-2000 J/kg range. Shear will \ncontinue to be lacking, but given the better thermodynamic profile, \nstorms on Monday and Tuesday could pose a slightly higher threat of \nbecoming strong to severe with damaging winds still the primary \nthreat. \n\nFor Wednesday onward, a ridge over the central US and a broad trough \nover the western Atlantic will keep our area in a NW flow. Some \ndisturbances in the flow and/or upstream MCS remnants may bring \nchances of showers and storms each day, with the northern areas \nfavored for the higher rain chances and potential for strong storms. \nA frontal boundary may set up south of the OH Valley later in the \nweek, possibly into southern WV, which could be the focus for some \nheavy rain and flooding concerns around Friday.",
